from expand_common import *
f=family; F=Fraction
t='a_model'
f(t,'decision-leaves','Information needed to distinguish permutations',r'A comparison sorting decision tree distinguishes all orderings of $n$ distinct keys. Find the minimum integer height allowed by its leaf-count bound.',r'$\lceil\log_2(n!)\rceil$',[r'$\lfloor\log_2(n!)\rfloor$',r'$n^2$',r'$n\lceil\log_2n\rceil$'],lambda n:(math.factorial(n)-1).bit_length(),[lambda n:math.factorial(n).bit_length()-1,lambda n:n*n,lambda n:n*(n-1).bit_length()],r'The tree needs at least $n!$ distinguishable leaves. A binary tree of height $h$ has at most $2^h$ leaves, so its integer height is at least the ceiling of the binary logarithm. This is a lower bound, not a construction of an optimal sorting algorithm.','The leaf-count bound applies to comparison-based sorting of distinct keys.',['It proves that every sorting algorithm uses only that many comparisons.','It prohibits linear-time integer sorting under stronger key operations.','Equal-key permutations always remain distinct required outcomes.'],'Key operations and distinguishable outcomes determine the decision model. Integer sorting can use operations not represented by binary comparisons, while repeated keys reduce distinct orderings. The information bound must match the allowed observations.')
f(t,'explicit-subsets','Enumerating objects versus emitting their descriptions',r'All subsets of an $n$-element set are emitted as full $n$-bit membership vectors. Count emitted bits.',r'$n2^n$',[r'$2^n$',r'$n^2$',r'$2^{n^2}$'],lambda n:n*2**n,[lambda n:2**n,lambda n:n*n,lambda n:2**(n*n)],r'There are $2^n$ subsets, and the stipulated representation writes $n$ bits for each. Multiply the number of outputs by the required length of each output. Counting just the objects ignores the explicit-output cost.','Compressed or incremental representations require a different output-cost model.',['Changing representation never changes output cost.','A unit-cost word always holds arbitrarily many bits.','Emitting exponentially many objects costs only the logarithm of their number.'],'A full membership vector has a stipulated length. An incremental representation might write a changed position rather than a whole vector, but it is a different interface. Neither representation can be substituted silently when establishing the stated cost.')
f(t,'bit-length','Numeric magnitude is not binary input size',r'The positive integer $2^n-1$ is represented in ordinary binary without leading zeros. Find its representation length.',r'$n$',[r'$2^n-1$',r'$n-1$',r'$n+1$'],lambda n:n,[lambda n:2**n-1,lambda n:n-1,lambda n:n+1],r'The integer lies between $2^{n-1}$ and $2^n-1$, so its leading one-bit occupies position $n-1$. All lower positions also contain ones. Binary length is the floor of the logarithm plus one, not the value of the integer.','A loop bounded by the integer value may be exponential in binary input length.',['Numeric value and input length are always equal.','Every linear-value algorithm is polynomial in binary length.','Leading zero padding determines the minimal representation length.'],'Running one step per numeric unit gives nearly $2^n$ iterations on an input with only $n$ bits. Complexity must be expressed in the size of the encoded input. Artificial leading zeros affect a supplied representation but not this minimal-length convention.')
f(t,'unary-size','Encoding-dependent growth',r'The same positive integer $2^n-1$ is encoded in unary with one mark per unit. Count marks.',r'$2^n-1$',[r'$n$',r'$2^n$',r'$2^{n-1}$'],lambda n:2**n-1,[lambda n:n,lambda n:2**n,lambda n:2**(n-1)],r'Unary uses the numeric value as its length, unlike binary. Each unit contributes exactly one mark. The two encodings therefore give dramatically different relationships between numeric-value bounds and input-size bounds.','Polynomiality must state the representation whose size is being measured.',['A polynomial in numeric value is polynomial in every encoding.','Binary and unary lengths differ by a fixed additive constant.','Encoding never affects a complexity classification.'],'The unary representation can be exponentially longer than the binary representation of the same value. An algorithm linear in value is linear in unary length but generally exponential in binary length. The data encoding is part of the computational model.')
f(t,'matrix-multiplications','Scalar operations in the standard matrix product',r'Multiply two dense $n$-by-$n$ matrices using the direct row-column algorithm. Count scalar multiplications.',r'$n^3$',[r'$n^2$',r'$n^2(n-1)$',r'$2n^3$'],lambda n:n**3,[lambda n:n*n,lambda n:n*n*(n-1),lambda n:2*n**3],r'There are $n^2$ output entries. Each dot product contains $n$ scalar products. Multiplying these counts gives the multiplication total; the addition total is different because a dot product adds together its already computed terms.','A scalar-operation count does not automatically give a bit-operation count.',['Every arbitrarily large integer multiplication costs one bit operation.','The number of additions equals the number of outputs.','The direct algorithm proves no faster matrix algorithm can exist.'],'Operand precision controls the cost of a scalar operation in a bit model. The direct algorithm establishes its own operation count, not an unconditional lower bound. Algebraic algorithms can reorganize the computation and reduce the number of multiplications.')
f(t,'permutation-output','Explicit permutation enumeration',r'Emit every permutation of $n$ distinct labels as a length-$n$ list. Count emitted labels.',r'$n\,n!$',[r'$n!$',r'$n^n$',r'$n^2$'],lambda n:n*math.factorial(n),[lambda n:math.factorial(n),lambda n:n**n,lambda n:n*n],r'Permutations are bijective ordered arrangements, so there are $n!$ outputs. The specified interface emits all $n$ labels each time. Multiplying output count by output length gives an unavoidable writing cost for that interface.','Counting all functions would permit repetition and would not count permutations.',['There are as many permutations as arbitrary self-maps.','The output size can be ignored for explicit enumeration.','Two permutations differing in order are the same output.'],'A permutation uses every label exactly once, whereas a self-map can repeat images. Explicit output imposes its own work even when generating the next permutation internally is cheap. The list order is essential to the identity of each permutation.')
f(t,'triangular-comparisons','An algorithm with a triangular operation region',r'For each position $i=1,\ldots,n$, compare its key once with every earlier position. Count comparisons.',r'$n(n-1)/2$',[r'$n(n+1)/2$',r'$n^2$',r'$n-1$'],lambda n:n*(n-1)//2,[lambda n:n*(n+1)//2,lambda n:n*n,lambda n:n-1],r'The inner work at position $i$ is $i-1$, not a fixed $n$. Sum the integers from zero through $n-1$. Each unordered pair of positions is compared exactly once and self-comparisons are excluded.','An early-exit modification would need its own best- and worst-case analysis.',['Every nested loop automatically has a rectangular iteration region.','A self-comparison is included for every position here.','The exact count is determined only by the number of loops.'],'Bounds and control flow define the iteration region. The present algorithm performs all stipulated comparisons. Introducing a data-dependent exit may reduce some executions, but the worst case must still be justified using a suitable input rather than inferred from syntax alone.')
f(t,'search-height','Comparisons versus the number of stored keys',r'A perfect binary search tree stores $2^n-1$ distinct keys. Count comparisons in the deepest successful search.',r'$n$',[r'$2^n-1$',r'$n-1$',r'$n+1$'],lambda n:n,[lambda n:2**n-1,lambda n:n-1,lambda n:n+1],r'The root is depth zero and the deepest keys have depth $n-1$. A successful search compares at every visited node, so the path contains $n$ key comparisons. The edge count is one less than the node count.','The logarithmic bound uses the tree-height assumption.',['Every binary search tree with this many nodes is perfect.','Searching an arbitrary unbalanced tree has the same height.','The empty final position adds a comparison to every successful search.'],'A degenerate binary search tree can have a linear-height path. Perfection gives the balanced level count in this question. A successful search stops at its matching node; an unsuccessful search may additionally visit an empty structural position without comparing another key.')

t='a_asym'
f(t,'polynomial-ratio','An exact ratio before taking a limit',r'For $F(n)=3n^2+5n$, calculate $F(n)/n^2$.',r'$3+5/n$',[r'$3$',r'$8$',r'$3+5n$'],lambda n:F(3)+F(5,n),[lambda n:3,lambda n:8,lambda n:3+5*n],r'Divide every term by the same normalizing power. The lower-degree term becomes $5/n$, which tends to zero but remains present in every exact finite ratio. The limiting constant supports a tight quadratic bound because it is positive and finite.','A limiting ratio is not necessarily the exact finite ratio.',['Lower-order terms are zero at every finite size.','A positive finite ratio limit gives only an upper bound.','A zero ratio limit proves the numerator is identically zero.'],'Limits describe eventual behavior. A positive finite limit gives both upper and lower constant bounds for all sufficiently large sizes. A zero limit gives little-o behavior, while exact arithmetic still retains any nonzero lower-order terms.')
f(t,'exponential-ratio','The larger exponential base dominates',r'For $F(n)=3^n+2^n$, calculate $F(n)/3^n$.',r'$1+(2/3)^n$',[r'$(2/3)^n$',r'$2$',r'$1+(3/2)^n$'],lambda n:F(1)+F(2,3)**n,[lambda n:F(2,3)**n,lambda n:2,lambda n:F(1)+F(3,2)**n],r'Normalize by the larger exponential. The first term becomes one and the second has a base strictly below one, so it decays to zero. Keep the fraction in its proper orientation: reversing the base changes decay into growth.','Different exponential bases are generally not interchangeable up to a constant factor.',['Every exponential with a fixed base has the same asymptotic order.','Adding a smaller positive term destroys the larger term lower bound.','The ratio of the two bases should be reversed to find the limit.'],'For two bases greater than one, their quotient raised to the input size controls relative growth. If that quotient exceeds one it is unbounded. The larger exponential is a lower bound for the sum and, eventually, a constant-factor upper bound as well.')
f(t,'log-log-input','Nested logarithms of a doubly exponential size',r'Let $N=2^{2^n}$. Calculate $\log_2\log_2N$.',r'$n$',[r'$2^n$',r'$n^2$',r'$n+1$'],lambda n:n,[lambda n:2**n,lambda n:n*n,lambda n:n+1],r'Take the logarithms one at a time. The first returns the inner exponent $2^n$; the second returns $n$. Replacing two logarithms by a square of one logarithm would produce a completely different function.','A squared logarithm is different from an iterated logarithm.',['Two logarithms mean the square of the first logarithm.','Changing a fixed logarithm base makes polynomial and exponential growth interchangeable.','The first logarithm already equals the innermost exponent index.'],'Iteration applies the operation to the output of the preceding operation. A fixed-base change multiplies a logarithm by a constant, but squaring or iterating changes the operation itself. Parentheses determine which growth class is being discussed.')
f(t,'harmonic','Exact partial sums and a logarithmic growth class',r'Calculate $H_n=\sum_{k=1}^{n}1/k$.',r'$\sum_{k=1}^{n}1/k$',[r'$n$',r'$1/n$',r'$(n+1)/2$'],lambda n:sum((F(1,k) for k in range(1,n+1)),F(0)),[lambda n:n,lambda n:F(1,n),lambda n:F(n+1,2)],r'The exact quantity is a sum of distinct reciprocals; use a common denominator or exact rational arithmetic. Integral comparison sandwiches the sum between logarithms differing by a constant. Those bounds prove logarithmic growth without claiming an exact logarithmic identity.','Asymptotic equivalence does not permit replacing an exact finite sum by its growth class.',['The harmonic sum equals the logarithm exactly.','Each reciprocal has the same value.','A divergent sum must grow linearly.'],'The individual terms decrease and the integral comparison accumulates this decay. Divergence only means unbounded growth, not a particular rate. Exact rational evaluation and asymptotic bounds answer different questions and must not be substituted for one another.')
f(t,'subtracted-lower','A positive difference with a lower-order subtraction',r'For $N=n^2$, evaluate $(N-\sqrt N)/N$.',r'$1-1/n$',[r'$1+1/n$',r'$n-1$',r'$1/n$'],lambda n:F(1)-F(1,n),[lambda n:F(1)+F(1,n),lambda n:n-1,lambda n:F(1,n)],r'For positive $n$, the square root of $n^2$ is $n$. Divide the two numerator terms by $n^2$ to obtain one minus the reciprocal. The ratio tends to one and is bounded away from zero for sufficiently large sizes.','Subtracting a lower-order term can preserve a tight bound when eventual positivity is proved.',['Every subtraction invalidates a lower bound.','A leading-term cancellation is always harmless.','A negative expression is a valid positive running time without qualification.'],'The result stays a positive constant fraction of the larger term eventually. This differs from subtracting two equal leading terms, where cancellation changes the order. Tight runtime bounds must respect both eventual magnitude and nonnegativity.')
f(t,'positive-polynomials','Bounding a sum by its maximum term',r'Calculate $(n^3+n^2)/n^3$.',r'$1+1/n$',[r'$1+1/n^2$',r'$2$',r'$n+1$'],lambda n:F(1)+F(1,n),[lambda n:F(1)+F(1,n*n),lambda n:2,lambda n:n+1],r'Divide termwise by $n^3$. Because both terms are nonnegative, the sum lies between its largest term and twice that term when the smaller term is no larger. The exact normalized lower-order contribution is $1/n$.','The maximum-term rule for sums relies on a fixed number of nonnegative terms.',['Cancellation cannot affect the largest-term rule.','A growing number of terms never changes the total order.','An upper bound automatically supplies a matching lower bound.'],'Opposite-sign terms may cancel, and a number of terms growing with the input can introduce another factor. Here there are exactly two positive terms, so both sides of the constant-factor sandwich follow directly.')
f(t,'oscillating','Tight order without a ratio limit',r'Let $F(k)=k(2+(-1)^k)$. Calculate $F(2n)$.',r'$6n$',[r'$2n$',r'$3n$',r'$4n$'],lambda n:6*n,[lambda n:2*n,lambda n:3*n,lambda n:4*n],r'The substituted argument is even, so its sign term equals one. Multiplying the argument $2n$ by three gives $6n$. For arbitrary positive arguments the multiplier alternates between one and three, providing constant upper and lower bounds.','A function can be tightly linear even when its ratio to the input has no limit.',['A tight bound requires a unique ratio limit.','The alternating multiplier makes the function negative.','An even-subsequence value proves the same exact value on odd inputs.'],'Tight order needs eventual constant bounds, not convergence of a normalized ratio. The odd subsequence has multiplier one and the even subsequence multiplier three. Both remain within a fixed positive interval, despite their different limits.')
f(t,'factorial-information','A factorial leaf count and its integer logarithm',r'Find the smallest integer $h$ such that $2^h\ge n!$.',r'$\lceil\log_2(n!)\rceil$',[r'$\lfloor\log_2(n!)\rfloor$',r'$n^2$',r'$n\lceil\log_2n\rceil$'],lambda n:(math.factorial(n)-1).bit_length(),[lambda n:math.factorial(n).bit_length()-1,lambda n:n*n,lambda n:n*(n-1).bit_length()],r'The defining inequality gives a ceiling logarithm. For exact evaluation compare neighboring integer powers with the factorial. For growth, at least half the factorial factors are at least $n/2$, while every factor is at most $n$, giving tight order $n\log n$.','A bound on the logarithm of a factorial is not a bound on the factorial itself.',['Taking a logarithm preserves every growth order.','The floor always meets a greater-than-or-equal power threshold.','Factorial growth is polynomial because its logarithm has polynomial size.'],'Logarithms compress products into sums and can radically change growth classes. The ceiling is required by the integer threshold. A polynomial-size representation or logarithm of a quantity does not imply that enumerating that many objects takes polynomial time.')

t='a_loop'
f(t,'double-triangle','A growing inner-loop length',r'For $i=1,\ldots,n$, an inner loop executes exactly $2i$ times. Count body executions.',r'$n(n+1)$',[r'$n^2$',r'$2n^2$',r'$n(n+1)/2$'],lambda n:n*(n+1),[lambda n:n*n,lambda n:2*n*n,lambda n:n*(n+1)//2],r'Sum the actual row lengths rather than multiplying two maximum bounds. The sum is twice the ordinary arithmetic prefix sum. This yields an exact count and explains why a rectangular approximation loses lower-order terms.','Changing the inner bound changes the iteration region even when the nesting depth stays the same.',['Two nested loops always execute the body exactly the input size squared times.','A maximum inner length is the length of every row.','An exact count and an asymptotic bound are the same statement.'],'The shape of the admissible index set controls work. Summing row lengths is exact; multiplying the outer count by a maximum inner length generally supplies only an upper bound. Asymptotic notation can conceal the difference but cannot replace an exact requested count.')
f(t,'square-inner','A polynomially expanding inner region',r'For $i=1,\ldots,n$, execute the inner body $i^2$ times. Count total body executions.',r'$n(n+1)(2n+1)/6$',[r'$n^3$',r'$n(n+1)/2$',r'$n^2$'],lambda n:n*(n+1)*(2*n+1)//6,[lambda n:n**3,lambda n:n*(n+1)//2,lambda n:n*n],r'The cost is the sum of squares of outer indices. The standard identity follows by induction or a telescoping cube difference. Each row must be included with its own squared length, not with a linear or fixed-length surrogate.','A variable exponent in a loop bound requires analysis beyond counting loop levels.',['Every two-loop program has quadratic work.','The inner squared bound can be replaced by its square root.','The body count is the largest single-row count.'],'Two loops can execute cubic work because the inner range grows quadratically with the outer index. Nesting depth alone does not determine a polynomial degree. The sum over all rows, rather than the cost of the last row alone, is the total.')
f(t,'geometric-rows','Doubling outer scales with linear inner work',r'At outer stages $j=0,\ldots,n$, execute the inner body $2^j$ times. Count total executions.',r'$2^{n+1}-1$',[r'$n2^n$',r'$2^n-1$',r'$n^2$'],lambda n:2**(n+1)-1,[lambda n:n*2**n,lambda n:2**n-1,lambda n:n*n],r'The row lengths form a geometric series including the stage-zero row. A finite geometric sum gives one less than the next power. Multiplying the number of stages by the last row length ignores the rapidly changing sizes.','A logarithmic number of stages can still have work proportional to the largest numeric scale.',['Logarithmically many outer iterations imply logarithmic total work.','Every stage has the final stage size.','The stage-zero body contributes nothing.'],'Stage count measures only one part of the computation. If work per stage doubles, the final few stages dominate the sum. A unit-cost body with one execution at stage zero also affects the exact boundary constant.')
f(t,'divisor-region','Counting a condition by reversing index roles',r'For all $i,j\in\{1,\ldots,n\}$, execute the body only when $j$ divides $i$. Count successful body executions.',r'$\sum_{j=1}^{n}\lfloor n/j\rfloor$',[r'$n^2$',r'$n(n+1)/2$',r'$n$'],lambda n:sum(n//j for j in range(1,n+1)),[lambda n:n*n,lambda n:n*(n+1)//2,lambda n:n],r'Fix the divisor index first. Its legal target indices are its multiples up to the bound, numbering the floor of the quotient. Summing over divisor indices counts each successful ordered pair exactly once. The full pair-checking loop still has a different total test cost.','Successful-body count differs from the number of condition checks.',['A skipped body also skips evaluation of its condition.','The body count equals the rectangular test count.','Reversing the order of counting changes the set of successful pairs.'],'The stated double loop evaluates the divisibility test on every pair, but the body runs only for successful pairs. These are distinct primitive operations. Reversing the counting viewpoint preserves the mathematical set while making its size easier to calculate.')
f(t,'continue-filter','A filter that skips one residue class',r'A loop visits integers one through $3n$ and skips its body exactly when the index is divisible by three. Count body executions.',r'$2n$',[r'$3n$',r'$n$',r'$2n-1$'],lambda n:2*n,[lambda n:3*n,lambda n:n,lambda n:2*n-1],r'Each complete block of three indices has one skipped value and two executed values. There are exactly $n$ complete blocks. The count includes the two nonmultiple indices in each block and excludes the final multiple at the upper endpoint.','A continue statement skips the remaining body, not necessarily the loop update.',['Continue always terminates the loop.','A for-loop update is always skipped by continue.','A skipped iteration is identical to a missing index value.'],'The control-flow semantics determine which later operations occur. A for-loop continue normally proceeds to its update expression, while a while-loop update located later in the body can be skipped. A count must therefore specify the actual loop structure and body checkpoint.')
f(t,'monotone-triple','Three correlated index ranges',r'Count executions indexed by $1\le i\le j\le k\le n$.',r'$\binom{n+2}{3}$',[r'$\binom n3$',r'$n^3$',r'$n(n+1)/2$'],lambda n:C(n+2,3),[lambda n:C(n,3),lambda n:n**3,lambda n:n*(n+1)//2],r'Each nondecreasing triple is a size-three multiset drawn from the allowed indices. Repetition is permitted, giving a combinations-with-repetition count. Alternatively sum the permitted third-index counts over each valid first and second index.','Strict inequalities would count triples without repetition.',['The weak inequalities force all three indices to differ.','Every rectangular index triple is valid.','An index equality violates the weak-order condition.'],'Weak ordering includes repeated values and its counting formula adds separator capacity. Replacing all inequalities by strict ones removes repeated-index triples and changes the count to an ordinary choice of three distinct positions.')
f(t,'logarithmic-grid','Correlated logarithmic loop lengths',r'At stages $i=0,\ldots,n-1$, start a second counter at $2^i$ and double it while it is at most $2^n$. Count all body executions.',r'$n(n+3)/2$',[r'$n(n+1)/2$',r'$n^2$',r'$2^n$'],lambda n:n*(n+3)//2,[lambda n:n*(n+1)//2,lambda n:n*n,lambda n:2**n],r'For a fixed stage, the visited exponents are $i,i+1$ through $n$, so the row length is $n-i+1$. Summing these lengths gives the descending arithmetic sum from $n+1$ down to two. Inclusive bounds contribute the extra endpoint at every stage.','The numeric doubling scale is different from the count of doublings.',['Doubling a counter visits every intermediate integer.','The inclusive final power contributes no iteration.','Every stage starts from the same counter value.'],'Take logarithms of the counter values to count iterations, then retain the specified starting and terminal exponents. The inner start depends on the outer index, so the exponent grid is triangular rather than rectangular.')
f(t,'merge-pointer','Comparisons along two monotone pointers',r'Merge two sorted lists of length $n$, with distinct keys, by comparing their current heads until one list empties. Find the worst-case key-comparison count.',r'$2n-1$',[r'$2n$',r'$n^2$',r'$n-1$'],lambda n:2*n-1,[lambda n:2*n,lambda n:n*n,lambda n:n-1],r'Every comparison outputs one key and advances one pointer. Before the final comparison both lists remain nonempty; at most all but one of the $2n$ keys can be output that way. Interleaving keys can force the last emptying step to occur at that bound.','Appending the leftover suffix does not require another key comparison.',['The remaining suffix always needs comparisons against itself.','Two pointers imply a quadratic number of comparisons.','The upper bound requires comparing every pair of keys.'],'Both pointers move only forward, so comparisons consume keys rather than revisit pairs. Once one input empties, the other suffix is already ordered. Counting copying or output operations may add work, but it must not be mislabeled as head-key comparisons.')

t='a_recurrence'
f(t,'affine-geometric','A linear recurrence with a constant toll',r'Let $T_0=0$ and $T_n=2T_{n-1}+3$. Calculate $T_n$.',r'$3(2^n-1)$',[r'$3\cdot2^n$',r'$2^n-1$',r'$3n$'],lambda n:3*(2**n-1),[lambda n:3*2**n,lambda n:2**n-1,lambda n:3*n],r'Add three to both sides to transform the recurrence into pure doubling for the shifted variable. Its initial shifted value is three. Undo the shift after taking the power; forgetting it loses the exact base-case information.','A homogeneous recurrence needs the shifted initial value, not the original one.',['The constant toll can always be omitted in an exact solution.','The original zero seed remains the shifted seed.','Repeated doubling with a positive toll gives linear growth.'],'Shifting removes the affine term but changes the boundary value at the same time. The closed form must satisfy both the recurrence and the original initial condition. Checking only one of these conditions leaves an undetermined or incorrect constant.')
f(t,'balanced-linear','Equal children and a linear combine toll',r'Let $U_0=1$ and $U_n=2U_{n-1}+2^n$. This represents input sizes $2^n$. Calculate $U_n$.',r'$(n+1)2^n$',[r'$n2^n$',r'$2^{n+1}-1$',r'$4^n$'],lambda n:(n+1)*2**n,[lambda n:n*2**n,lambda n:2**(n+1)-1,lambda n:4**n],r'Divide the recurrence by $2^n$. The normalized sequence increases by one at every level and starts at one, giving $n+1$. Multiplying back restores the original scale. Every full recursion level has the same combine contribution.','The level index is the logarithm of the original input size.',['The depth variable and the original input size are identical.','Every linear toll gives linear total cost under two children.','The base-case leaf costs never contribute to the exact total.'],'The input shrinks by halves, so the number of levels is logarithmic in the original input. Equal work per level accumulates a logarithmic factor, and the leaf boundary supplies the extra normalized one in the exact formula.')
f(t,'leaf-dominance','Four children and a smaller combine term',r'Let $U_0=1$ and $U_n=4U_{n-1}+2^n$. Calculate $U_n$.',r'$2\cdot4^n-2^n$',[r'$4^n$',r'$(n+1)2^n$',r'$2\cdot4^n$'],lambda n:2*4**n-2**n,[lambda n:4**n,lambda n:(n+1)*2**n,lambda n:2*4**n],r'Unroll the recurrence. The initial contribution is $4^n$ and the combine contributions form a geometric sum with successive ratio one half after normalization. Summing the finite tail yields another $4^n-2^n$.','A dominant leaf term determines order but not the exact lower-order correction.',['A smaller combine toll contributes exactly zero.','The exact total is identical to its dominant term.','The number of children is irrelevant to the growth.'],'The toll does not alter the leading growth order here, yet it changes the leading constant and leaves a finite correction. Asymptotic classification and exact solution require different levels of accounting, especially when leaf multiplicity grows faster than the toll.')
f(t,'single-child-linear','One child with decreasing linear tolls',r'Let $U_0=1$ and $U_n=U_{n-1}+2^n$. Calculate $U_n$.',r'$2^{n+1}-1$',[r'$2^n$',r'$n2^n$',r'$n+1$'],lambda n:2**(n+1)-1,[lambda n:2**n,lambda n:n*2**n,lambda n:n+1],r'Accumulate all tolls from the first positive level through the final level and include the base one. The powers form a geometric series, so the final scale dominates without an extra depth factor. The exact sum retains its endpoint correction.','One child and two children need different level-work accounting.',['A logarithmic depth always multiplies the top toll by that depth.','Every recurrence with halved arguments has identical total work.','The base seed must be discarded because it is small.'],'One-child levels shrink their total work geometrically; two-child levels can retain equal aggregate work. The number of subproblems at each depth therefore matters as much as the per-subproblem toll when deciding whether a depth factor appears.')
f(t,'log-squared-toll','Summing a toll expressed in the level index',r'Let $U_0=1$ and $U_n=U_{n-1}+n^2$. Calculate $U_n$.',r'$1+n(n+1)(2n+1)/6$',[r'$n(n+1)(2n+1)/6$',r'$n^3$',r'$n^2+1$'],lambda n:1+n*(n+1)*(2*n+1)//6,[lambda n:n*(n+1)*(2*n+1)//6,lambda n:n**3,lambda n:n*n+1],r'Unroll into the base term plus the sum of square tolls. The square-sum identity includes every positive level. If this index is a logarithm of an original input, the resulting cubic growth is a cube of that logarithm rather than a cube of input size.','A change of variables must also transform the interpretation of the solution.',['The transformed level index is always the original input size.','Summing a squared level toll preserves quadratic growth.','Changing variables permits omission of base cases.'],'The normalized recurrence is easier to solve, but its index retains a specific relationship to input size. Translating back is part of the solution. Forgetting this final step produces a dramatically wrong asymptotic class even when the recurrence algebra is correct.')
f(t,'subtract-one','A decrement recurrence and polynomial toll',r'Let $T_0=0$ and $T_n=T_{n-1}+n^2$. Calculate $T_n$.',r'$n(n+1)(2n+1)/6$',[r'$n^3$',r'$n^2$',r'$n(n+1)/2$'],lambda n:n*(n+1)*(2*n+1)//6,[lambda n:n**3,lambda n:n*n,lambda n:n*(n+1)//2],r'The argument decreases by one rather than by a fixed fraction. Thus unrolling has one toll for every positive integer up to the input. Their sum is the square-sum expression; a balanced-recursion theorem cannot be applied by merely matching the toll.','The standard Master theorem does not cover a subtract-one argument.',['Any recursive expression can be inserted into the Master theorem.','A decrement by one gives logarithmic recursion depth.','The top toll alone is the total exact cost.'],'The Master theorem concerns fixed-factor shrinkage with its stated hypotheses. A subtract-one recurrence has linear depth, and its accumulated polynomial toll gains another power. Unrolling or induction directly verifies the correct solution.')
f(t,'critical-level-index','Two children with a logarithmic toll',r'Let $U_0=0$ and $U_n=2U_{n-1}+n$. Calculate $U_n$.',r'$2^{n+1}-n-2$',[r'$2^{n+1}$',r'$2^n$',r'$2^{n+1}-1$'],lambda n:2**(n+1)-n-2,[lambda n:2**(n+1),lambda n:2**n,lambda n:2**(n+1)-1],r'Normalize by $2^n$ and sum the terms $j/2^j$. The finite weighted geometric sum equals $2-(n+2)/2^n$. Multiply back and retain both boundary corrections. Substitution into the recurrence supplies an independent algebraic check.','Finite weighted sums retain corrections absent from their infinite limits.',['Replacing a finite sum by its infinite limit gives the exact same result.','A logarithmic toll always adds a logarithmic factor to the dominant leaf term.','The initial zero condition is irrelevant.'],'The infinite weighted sum gives the leading coefficient, while its remaining tail produces the exact correction. The branching rate can dominate the logarithmic toll, so a logarithmic factor does not automatically survive in the final growth class.')
f(t,'unequal-scale','Two children with input powers of three',r'Let $U_0=0$ and $U_n=2U_{n-1}+1$, representing inputs of size $3^n$. Calculate $U_n$.',r'$2^n-1$',[r'$3^n-1$',r'$n$',r'$2^n$'],lambda n:2**n-1,[lambda n:3**n-1,lambda n:n,lambda n:2**n],r'Unrolling gives a binary recursion tree with one unit at each internal level contribution. Sum the powers of two. For the original input variable, the leading factor is its power with exponent $\log_3 2$, not a linear function of that input.','Branch count and shrink factor are separate parameters.',['Two children imply a base-three exponential in the level index.','Shrinking by three implies three children.','A recurrence exponent must always be an integer.'],'The level depth comes from the shrink factor and the number of nodes per level comes from the branch count. Combining these gives a possibly noninteger power of input size. Equating those two parameters loses the characteristic exponent.')
f(t,'square-root-scale','A nonlinear shrink transformed by two logarithms',r'At original input sizes $N=2^{2^n}$, the transformed cost satisfies $U_0=1$ and $U_n=2U_{n-1}+2^n$. Calculate $U_n$.',r'$(n+1)2^n$',[r'$n2^n$',r'$4^n$',r'$2^{n+1}-1$'],lambda n:(n+1)*2**n,[lambda n:n*2**n,lambda n:4**n,lambda n:2**(n+1)-1],r'The original square-root shrink lowers the outer exponent index by one. The normalized cost increases by one at each transformed level. Here $2^n$ is the logarithm of the original input, and $n$ is its iterated logarithm; translate both factors back.','A square-root recurrence cannot be analyzed by pretending its shrink ratio is a constant.',['The square root removes half the same fixed number of input elements.','The transformed index equals the square root of the input.','A logarithmic factor in the transformed variable is always a logarithmic factor in the original variable.'],'The ratio of the square root to the input changes with size. A logarithmic change of variables converts this nonlinear shrink to a fixed-factor shrink, but that conversion also changes the toll and the meaning of depth. Both transformations must be tracked.')

t='a_divide'
f(t,'merge-total','Exact worst-case comparisons across a balanced merge tree',r'Merge-sort $2^n$ distinct keys with balanced splits. Find the worst-case total key comparisons using merges with worst cost equal to combined length minus one.',r'$n2^n-2^n+1$',[r'$n2^n$',r'$2^n-1$',r'$n^2$'],lambda n:n*2**n-2**n+1,[lambda n:n*2**n,lambda n:2**n-1,lambda n:n*n],r'At a depth with $2^j$ merges, total compared lengths sum to $2^n$ and each merge saves one comparison. Sum the level costs $2^n-2^j$ for all nonleaf depths. A recursively interleaved ordering realizes the worst costs.','Charging combine work only once omits the work at other levels.',['All merges together cost only the final merge cost.','Every merge needs a comparison for its final leftover key.','The exact cost equals the dominant asymptotic expression.'],'Every nonleaf level combines a complete set of subarrays, so all levels contribute. Each merge stops comparing when one input empties; this subtracts one per merge in the worst case and accounts for the finite correction.')
f(t,'cross-inversions','Cross terms in an inversion-counting combine step',r'Two length-$n$ blocks are individually sorted, but every key in the left block exceeds every key in the right block. Count cross-block inversions.',r'$n^2$',[r'$n(n-1)$',r'$2n^2$',r'$4n^2$'],lambda n:n*n,[lambda n:n*(n-1),lambda n:2*n*n,lambda n:4*n*n],r'Every left position forms an inversion with every right position, creating a Cartesian product of the two index sets. There are no within-block inversions because each block is sorted. Cross-pair counting is separate from the comparisons used to detect the pairs.','A linear-time combine can count quadratically many inversions without listing each pair.',['Every counted inversion must be emitted as a separate pair.','The inversion count equals the merge comparison count.','Individually sorted blocks forbid cross-block inversions.'],'When a right key is smaller than the remaining left keys, add the number of remaining left positions at once. This aggregates many inversions into one arithmetic update. The count of objects and the cost of computing that count need not be the same.')
f(t,'karatsuba-leaves','Recursive arithmetic under three-way product reuse',r'A product algorithm on input width $2^n$ replaces each nonbase multiplication by three half-width multiplications. Count leaf multiplications.',r'$3^n$',[r'$4^n$',r'$2^n$',r'$n3^n$'],lambda n:3**n,[lambda n:4**n,lambda n:2**n,lambda n:n*3**n],r'There are three children per multiplication node and $n$ reductions to the base width. Induction gives three to the depth leaves. This counts leaf products; additions, subtractions and growing-precision arithmetic require separate cost accounting.','Leaf count is not automatically total bit complexity.',['Arithmetic additions are always free in every model.','Reusing products makes all precision costs constant.','The number of half-width subproducts remains four.'],'The method reduces the number of recursive products by algebraic reuse. Combining subproducts still requires arithmetic on relevant-width operands. A full complexity proof must account for that toll and state whether operations or bits are being counted.')
f(t,'strassen-leaves','Seven subproducts in a matrix recursion',r'A matrix algorithm halves each dimension and makes seven recursive subproducts. Starting at dimension $2^n$, count scalar-product leaves.',r'$7^n$',[r'$8^n$',r'$4^n$',r'$2^n$'],lambda n:7**n,[lambda n:8**n,lambda n:4**n,lambda n:2**n],r'Each reduction creates seven product calls, and the dimension reaches one after $n$ reductions. The leaf tree therefore has $7^n$ scalar multiplications. Translating back to matrix dimension gives exponent $\log_2 7$ for this product count.','Nonproduct additions still contribute a combine toll.',['Seven products imply only seven total scalar products.','Halving each dimension divides the number of recursion levels by four.','The leaf count proves every matrix algorithm has the same lower bound.'],'This is the operation count of one particular recursion design. It is neither a universal lower bound nor the entire implementation cost. The combine matrices need additions, and padding non-power-of-two dimensions also needs a stated convention.')
f(t,'convolution-length','Linear convolution versus circular aliasing',r'Two coefficient arrays have length $n$, with their last coefficients nonzero. Find their linear convolution length.',r'$2n-1$',[r'$2n$',r'$n^2$',r'$n$'],lambda n:2*n-1,[lambda n:2*n,lambda n:n*n,lambda n:n],r'Each polynomial has degree $n-1$. The product has degree $2n-2$ because its leading coefficient is nonzero. Including index zero gives one more coefficient than the degree, hence length $2n-1$.','An FFT length below the required linear length can introduce wraparound aliasing.',['Circular and linear convolution always coincide.','The number of coefficients equals the degree.','A transform of length the input length is always enough for the product.'],'The cyclic transform treats exponents modulo its length. To recover an ordinary product without overlap, embed the linear result in a transform at least as long as its coefficient length. Degree-to-length conversion must also include the zero coefficient index.')
f(t,'fft-butterflies','Counting butterflies by complete levels',r'A radix-two FFT has input length $2^n$ and $n$ stages, each with half as many butterflies as inputs. Count butterflies.',r'$n2^{n-1}$',[r'$n2^n$',r'$2^n-1$',r'$n^2$'],lambda n:n*2**(n-1),[lambda n:n*2**n,lambda n:2**n-1,lambda n:n*n],r'Every stage pairs all input positions, so it has $2^{n-1}$ butterflies. The stage count is $n$. Multiply these two independent counts. A butterfly performs several primitive arithmetic operations, which must not be confused with the butterfly count itself.','Transform normalization and the sign convention belong to correctness, not just operation counting.',['The same transform sign always serves forward and inverse transforms without correction.','Every butterfly is exactly one bit operation.','Dropping normalization never changes the inverse result.'],'A correct forward/inverse pair uses compatible exponential signs and scaling. The work count alone cannot detect a mathematically wrong transform convention. Arithmetic precision can also alter the bit cost per butterfly without changing the number of butterflies.')
f(t,'median-pivot','A guaranteed discard count for groups of five',r'Partition $10n+5$ distinct keys into $2n+1$ full groups of five. Choose the median of their medians. Find the guaranteed number of keys strictly smaller than that pivot.',r'$3n+2$',[r'$n+1$',r'$5n$',r'$3n+3$'],lambda n:3*n+2,[lambda n:n+1,lambda n:5*n,lambda n:3*n+3],r'There are $n$ group medians strictly below the chosen median. Each of those groups contributes at least three keys below the pivot. The pivot own group contributes its two lower keys. Together these give $3n+2$ keys guaranteed smaller.','Distinct keys and full groups are part of this exact bound.',['The pivot own group contributes three strictly smaller keys.','Every key in a below-median group is smaller than the pivot.','Incomplete groups require no correction to an exact discard count.'],'The pivot itself is not strictly smaller, and only the three lower-side positions of each other qualifying group are guaranteed. Equal keys or an incomplete final group change the strict-inequality and endpoint accounting; asymptotic discard fractions need separate exact qualifications.')
f(t,'binary-base','Recursive visits in an unsuccessful balanced search',r'An unsuccessful binary search descends through a perfect tree with $2^n-1$ stored keys and visits the final empty subtree as a base call. Count all visited calls.',r'$n+1$',[r'$n$',r'$2^n$',r'$n^2$'],lambda n:n+1,[lambda n:n,lambda n:2**n,lambda n:n*n],r'The path contains $n$ stored nodes and one final empty base case. The call count therefore exceeds the key-comparison count by one. The failure branch must be included when the question counts calls rather than only comparisons.','A recurrence base case can contribute work even when it holds no key.',['Every empty-subtree call compares a stored key.','A successful search always visits a final empty subtree.','The number of calls equals the number of nodes stored in the tree.'],'Calls, comparisons and stored nodes are different counted objects. The unsuccessful execution includes a terminal base call but no key comparison there. A successful search instead stops at a matching key and omits that final empty call.')
